---
// Canonical home of the brand usage policy (tracker#8).
//
// Moved here from backend/TRADEMARK.md, where it landed only because backend
// was the first repo to go public (backend#6). Its audience is mostly people
// who will never open a repository — someone naming a package, writing an
// article, or weighing up a fork — so it belongs on the site next to /privacy
// and /terms, with every repo linking this one URL instead of copying the text.
//
// NOT REVIEWED BY COUNSEL. The marks are unregistered; see the Status section.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED, SELFHOST_URL } from '../config';
---

<Base
  title="Trademark"
  description="How you may use the HomeAccounting name and logo: what is free, what needs a rebrand, and what is never allowed."
>
  <section class="container max-w-3xl py-20">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">Trademark and brand usage policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted-foreground">
      The HomeAccounting <strong>software</strong> is free and open source under
      the AGPL-3.0. The HomeAccounting <strong>name, logo and visual identity</strong
      > are not — they are trademarks of the project, and this page explains how
      you may use them.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Last updated {LAST_UPDATED}.</p>

    <p class="mt-6 text-muted-foreground">
      This is normal for open-source projects and is not a restriction on your
      software freedom. Its purpose is narrow: when someone sees the
      HomeAccounting name on a personal-finance application, they must be able
      to trust that it is this project, with this project&rsquo;s security
      posture. A fork that changes the code and keeps the name breaks that
      guarantee for everyone.
    </p>

    <div
      class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/40 p-6 text-muted-foreground"
    >
      <p class="font-semibold text-foreground">Status</p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        The HomeAccounting marks are <strong>unregistered</strong>, and no
        application is on file. This policy rests on unregistered rights —
        narrower than a registration, but real, and the same footing most
        open-source brand policies stand on. If that changes, this paragraph
        changes with it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">You may, without asking</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        State truthfully that your project, product or article <strong
          >works with</strong
        >, <strong>is built on</strong>, <strong>integrates with</strong> or
        <strong>is compatible with</strong> HomeAccounting.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use the name in prose to refer to this project — in documentation, blog
        posts, talks, comparisons, reviews and academic work, including critical
        ones.
      </li>
      <li>
        Run an <strong>unmodified</strong> build of HomeAccounting for yourself,
        your family, your company or your clients, and call it HomeAccounting.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use the logo to link to the project or to indicate an integration,
        without altering its colours, proportions or elements.
      </li>
      <li>Redistribute unmodified official builds and packages under the project name.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">You must rebrand if you</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        Distribute a <strong>modified</strong> version of HomeAccounting. Change
        the name, the logo and the visible branding. You keep every freedom the
        AGPL gives you over the code; you simply may not call the result
        HomeAccounting.
      </li>
      <li>
        Operate a <strong>hosted service</strong> based on HomeAccounting for
        third parties under a name containing &ldquo;HomeAccounting&rdquo;, or in
        a way that implies it is the official managed service.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      When you rebrand, please keep an accurate attribution such as &ldquo;based
      on HomeAccounting&rdquo; — that is encouraged, and is not a trademark use
      we object to.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">You may never</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        Use the name or logo in a way that suggests your project, service or
        company is official, endorsed by or affiliated with HomeAccounting when
        it is not.
      </li>
      <li>
        Register the name, a confusingly similar name, or the logo as your own
        trademark, company name or domain in a way that suggests official status.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use the marks on merchandise for sale, or in advertising for an unrelated
        product.
      </li>
      <li>
        Alter the logo, or combine it with other marks in a way that creates a
        new composite mark.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Domains, social accounts and package names</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      Do not register domain names, social-media handles or package-registry
      names containing &ldquo;homeaccounting&rdquo; in a way that implies you are
      the project. <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm"
        >homeaccounting-monzo-provider</code
      > as a community package is fine; <code
        class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm">homeaccounting-cloud.com</code
      > as a competing hosted service is not.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Questions and permission</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      This policy cannot anticipate everything, and we would rather say yes to a
      reasonable use than have you guess. Ask at <a
        class="underline"
        href="mailto:legal@homeaccounting.com">legal@homeaccounting.com</a
      >, or open a discussion. Permission granted in writing overrides anything
      above.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      We reserve the right to revise this policy; revisions will not be applied
      retroactively to uses already permitted in writing.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-14 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      The code itself is under the AGPL-3.0 and is yours to fork — <a
        class="underline"
        href={SELFHOST_URL}>run it yourself</a
      >, modify it, redistribute it. This page is only about the name.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
